Purpose-The purpose of this paper is to analyse, based on the new data, how often do contracting authorities in Croatia choose arbitration as a public procurement contract performance dispute mechanism as opposed to regular courts.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach-In this paper, the author applies legal, analytical and doctrinal methodology to give background on the availability of arbitration as a public procurement contract dispute resolution mechanism in EU directives and Member State legal systems, as well as in Croatia. The prevalence of arbitration clauses is researched based on 17,695 contract notices published in the Croatian Public Procurement Electronic Tenders Daily in 2025, where 6,409 draft contracts with arbitration clauses were acquired via data scrape.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings-Croatian administrative and public procurement law is based on French droit administratif, but diOers notably in defining public procurement contracts and allowing arbitration as a dispute resolution method. No special public law rules apply, so arbitration involving public bodies is subject to the same rules as private arbitration. Contracting authorities freely choose between arbitration and courts; although only 5.32% of collected draft contracts included arbitration clauses, this represents a notable share of public procurement draft contracts.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Social implications-This paper provides new insights into the decision-making of contracting authorities, especially when freely choosing between a transparent and lengthy dispute resolution mechanism versus an opaque and expedient one.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/value-To the best of the author's knowledge, this is the first study of the prevalence of arbitration clauses in public procurement draft contracts in Croatia, and the first study that gives insights into the contracting authorities preference when unconstrained by legal limits on arbitration use.
</div>

Accurate identification of agricultural pests is essential for reducing crop losses and enabling timely intervention in precision agriculture. Automated pest recognition remains difficult because of large intra-class variation, visual similarity among species, and complex field imaging conditions. Although recent deep-learning methods have improved classification performance, many high-capacity models remain computationally demanding for resource-constrained deployment. In particular, transformer-based vision models typically require substantially more parameters than conventional convolutional neural networks (CNNs), whereas lightweight CNNs such as MobileNet remain among the most efficient deployment baselines.&nbsp;
<div>
 This study evaluates parameter-efficient transformer architectures for largescale pest classification by integrating sparse training and magnitude-based pruning into Vision Transformer (ViT) and Swin Transformer models. The proposed sparse-then-prune pipeline applies ℓ1-based regularization to encourage sparse attention representations during training, followed by global magnitude pruning and fine-tuning to recover pruning-induced loss. Experiments are conducted on IP102 (102 classes; over 75,000 images) under the standard data partition and a unified training/evaluation protocol. ViT achieves a peak accuracy of 72.95%, while Swin provides a more balanced accuracy–efficiency trade-off. At pruning ratios up to 30%, Swin reduces parameter count by nearly 30% while maintaining 70.52% accuracy. These results indicate that sparsity-aware optimization can improve transformer resource efficiency while preserving competitive performance for large-scale pest recognition.&nbsp;
</div>

Objective: We created a model for a lung cancer advocacy network (LCAN) that engaged diverse advocates with complex relationships to lung cancer (LC) across disciplinary settings to address the challenges associated with the medical care and social complexity of LC.&nbsp;
<div>
 Methods: This qualitative research used participatory action research and constructivist grounded theory to create a LCAN conceptual model.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: Twenty-four LC advocates across disciplines participated in 7 focus groups and 4 interviews. Four major themes and a singular subtheme emerged from transcript analysis: a) historic stigma has broad implications for LC; b) discordant communication impacts LC care, c) the future of LC care is hopeful due to medical advances; d) a LCAN requires intentional organizational communication. Storytelling emerged as a subtheme of intentional organizational communication. Storytelling is a primary communication tool that can address the medical and psychosocial challenges of LC.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Innovation: This model provides an innovative framework for LCANs to engage diverse advocate-types and use storytelling as a primary organizational communication strategy to improve LC care.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusion: Our model promotes storytelling as integral to address medical and psychosocial challenges associated with LC care.&nbsp;
</div>
